  "resources": {
    "costumbres": {
      "c-092124a94c18dc2d": {
        "fuente": {
          "entradaId": "freeman-302",
          "modulo": "freeman-manners-customs"
        },
        "texto": "These shields were of a smaller size than those referred to in the sixteenth verse. The Hebrew magen is in some places rendered “buckler” (2 Sam. 22:31; 2 Chron. 23:9) and, on the other hand, buckler is sometimes the rendering of tsinnah. See note on 1 Samuel 17:7 (#253). While,…"
      },
      "c-6c4953d01964c8c0": {
        "fuente": {
          "entradaId": "freeman-301",
          "modulo": "freeman-manners-customs"
        },
        "texto": "The “target” here is different from the one spoken of in 1 Samuel 17:6 where see the note (#252). There it is kidon, a javelin; here it is tsinnah, a large shield, for the description of which see note on 1 Samuel 17:7 (#253). These great golden shields of Solomon were probably m…"
      },
      "c-de372943af3863b1": {
        "fuente": {
          "entradaId": "freeman-303",
          "modulo": "freeman-manners-customs"
        },
        "texto": "The body of the throne was probably of wood, entirely covered with ivory and gold, both being visible and relieving each other. Judging from the description given of this throne it must have been one of extraordinary magnificence. It had, by the two arms, lions such as are repres…"
      }
    },
